"A second family of paradoxes needs no racetrack, only a sentence. Consider: This sentence is false. If it is true, then what it says is the case, so it is false. If it is false, then what it says is not the case, so it is true. The ancient version is credited to Eubulides, a contemporary of Aristotle, and it is known as the Liar. It is tempting to dismiss the Liar as a trick, and for two thousand years most people did. Then, in the early twentieth century, the same structure of self-reference turned up in the foundations of mathematics. Bertrand Russell found it in the notion of a set, and Kurt Gödel used a relative of the Liar, a sentence that says of itself that it cannot be proved, to show that arithmetic contains truths no consistent system of rules can derive. Alfred Tarskis response to the Liar was that no language can contain its own truth predicate: to say of a sentence that it is true, one has to step up into a richer language. Ordinary English falls short of that standard, which may mean that ordinary English is, strictly speaking, inconsistent.": "016227015f13",
"A stick one foot long: take away half of it every day, and in ten thousand generations it will not be used up.": "a8d4f8bcb054",
"Achilles is always on the point of drawing level and never does.": "e9dd92246bdb",
"Around 200 CE the Indian philosopher Nāgārjuna opened the second chapter of his Fundamental Verses on the Middle Way with an argument that could be mistaken for Zenos. What has already been traversed is not being traversed. What has not yet been traversed is not being traversed. And apart from these two there is no third stretch, being traversed now, to be found. In Kumārajīvas Chinese: 已去無有去，未去亦無去，離已去未去，去時亦無去. Two centuries later his Chinese follower Sengzhao drew the conclusion in a famous line: the whirlwind that topples mountains is forever still, and the rivers that race to the sea do not flow.": "97078168e620",
"By the time Achilles has covered the hundred, the other has crept forward ten.": "85341b0749e0",
"Chinese thinkers found the same puzzle independently. The last chapter of the Zhuangzi records a saying of the debaters around Hui Shi, in the fourth century BCE: A stick one foot long: take away half of it every day, and in ten thousand generations it will not be used up. This is Zenos other great argument, the Dichotomy, turned from motion to matter. Before you reach the door you must reach the halfway point, and before that the halfway point of the first half. Run in one direction the argument says you never arrive, and run in the other it says you can never set out.": "b38407aeaa24",
"Eubulides is also credited with the paradox of the heap, the sorites. One grain of sand is not a heap. Adding a single grain to something that is not a heap never turns it into one. So no number of grains makes a heap. Each premise seems undeniable, the logic is modus ponens repeated, and the conclusion is false. What makes the sorites so hard is that the trouble is everywhere. Heap is vague, and so are tall, adult, alive, and person, and the same argument can be run on each of them. Law and ethics depend on such words, and on lines that have to be drawn somewhere although no particular place for them can be defended.": "6b6bffa5824a",
"Give the slow runner a start of a hundred meters. By the time Achilles has covered the hundred, the other has crept forward ten. By the time Achilles has covered those ten, it is one meter further on, and so without end. At every stage a gap remains. The stages never run out. So it seems that Achilles is always on the point of drawing level and never does.": "00d1a48d8443",
"Infinitely many distances need not add up to an infinite amount.": "25abd294b4e8",
"It would be a mistake to file all this under puzzles. A paradox is an argument that our own concepts make against us. It shows that what we believe about motion, or truth, or the meaning of ordinary words cannot all be right together, and it does so using nothing except those beliefs. The great ones are therefore never simply solved. They are answered by changing something: by inventing the limit, or separating a language from its metalanguage, or by rethinking what a set is. With this the first unit ends, on a caution. Logic can show us that our premises conflict. It does not tell us which of them to give up.": "5a7ce4b3d6c1",
"Many philosophers think this dissolves the paradox, and many do not. The arithmetic tells us where and when Achilles draws level. It does not obviously tell us how anyone finishes a sequence of tasks that has no last member. In 1954 James Thomson sharpened the worry with a lamp. Switch it on for a minute, off for half a minute, on for a quarter, and so on; after two minutes infinitely many switchings are done. Is the lamp then on or off? It cannot be on, since every time it was turned on it was turned off again, and it cannot be off for the same reason. Thomson concluded that completing infinitely many tasks, a supertask, is impossible. His critics replied that the story merely fails to say what happens at two minutes. The argument continues, and what is at stake in it is whether space, time, and motion are really as the mathematics of the continuum describes them.": "dbb2ed8e8aaa",
"Many philosophers think this dissolves the paradox.": "e96045a9007b",
"Ordinary English falls short of that standard.": "d8f29fd6fdf2",
"Philosophers call accepting the incredible conclusion biting the bullet.": "01f041090f48",
"Since a sound argument cannot have a false conclusion, something must give way.": "657ec1145348",
"The last two lessons were about telling good arguments from bad ones. A paradox is the case where that skill gives out. The premises look true. Each step looks valid. And the conclusion is something no sane person accepts: that the fastest runner cannot catch the slowest, that a sentence is true and false at once, that there is no such thing as a heap of sand. Since a sound argument cannot have a false conclusion, something must give way, and there are only three choices. One of the premises is false after all. Or one of the steps is invalid. Or the conclusion, incredible as it seems, is true. Philosophers call the third option biting the bullet, and the history of the subject is full of people who did.": "7cddb8a7c2ab",
"The oldest paradoxes we have come from Zeno of Elea, a Greek of the fifth century BCE. His teacher Parmenides had argued that reality is one and unchanging and that motion is an appearance. Zeno defended him by showing that the commonsense view, on which things move, leads to absurdities of its own. Aristotle preserves the most famous of his arguments: in a race the quickest runner can never overtake the slowest, since the pursuer must first reach the point whence the pursued started, so that the slower must always hold a lead.": "387b7831e339",
"The quickest runner can never overtake the slowest.": "8704ce3e3f0f",
"The resemblance is real and the purposes are opposite. Zeno argued against motion in order to defend Parmenides single unchanging reality. Nāgārjuna has no such reality to defend. His target is the assumption that a mover, a motion, and a path each exist with a nature of their own (svabhāva); grant that assumption, he argues, and motion becomes impossible, so the assumption has to go. People walk, conventionally speaking, and nothing in the argument denies it. Where Zeno accepts the conclusion and gives up change, Nāgārjuna uses the same kind of reductio to give up fixed essences, and that leaves him with a world in which there is nothing except change. Of the three responses to a paradox, he makes the first, and the premise he rejects is one that most of his readers had never noticed they held (Dharma and the West, D8 and D9).": "b0588f02cf48",
"The same structure turned up in the foundations of mathematics.": "ce2d4caa7efc",
"The second is the so-called Achilles, and it amounts to this, that in a race the quickest runner can never overtake the slowest, since the pursuer must first reach the point whence the pursued started, so that the slower must always hold a lead.": "17380fa5b490",
"The standard answer took two thousand years to state properly. Zeno assumes that infinitely many distances, or infinitely many intervals of time, must add up to an infinite amount. They need not. The distances in the race are 100, 10, 1, 0.1 and so on, and the more of them you add, the closer the total comes to 111.11 meters without ever exceeding it. Mathematicians of the nineteenth century, Cauchy foremost among them, made this exact with the concept of a limit. The times behave the same way. If Achilles runs ten meters a second, the stages take 10 seconds, then 1, then 0.1, and all of them together take 11.11 seconds. After that he is in front.": "d0a2c2b4c2b0",
"What is at stake is whether space and time are as the mathematics describes them.": "aa5fd7fb4efe",
